WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:07.172
Begin by framing Search Engine Architecture
Overview as a question about inputs,

2
00:00:07.172 --> 00:00:09.546
decisions, and observable results.

3
00:00:09.546 --> 00:00:18.417
Data systems organize records with declared
types, tables, rows, columns, keys, and

4
00:00:18.417 --> 00:00:19.156
constraints.

5
00:00:19.156 --> 00:00:28.008
Consider this worked case: A document database
stores text in tables with columns for title,

6
00:00:28.008 --> 00:00:34.499
content, and metadata, using primary keys to
uniquely identify each record.

7
00:00:34.499 --> 00:00:41.776
List the givens before choosing a method,
because an unstated assumption can change the

8
00:00:41.776 --> 00:00:42.296
conclusion.

9
00:00:42.296 --> 00:00:47.921
This establishes the vocabulary and scope
needed for the remaining sections.

10
00:00:48.026 --> 00:00:55.236
Now define Query Processing and Indexing
precisely enough that another learner could

11
00:00:55.236 --> 00:00:57.038
test the definition.

12
00:00:57.038 --> 00:01:02.507
Indexes provide additional access paths for
selected workloads.

13
00:01:02.507 --> 00:01:10.125
Consider this worked case: A term frequency
index stores each unique word alongside its

14
00:01:10.125 --> 00:01:17.200
document IDs, allowing the system to instantly
locate all documents containing a specific

15
00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:18.288
query term.

16
00:01:18.288 --> 00:01:24.911
Separate the formal definition from an
implementation detail, then identify what

17
00:01:24.911 --> 00:01:27.319
evidence would distinguish them.

18
00:01:27.319 --> 00:01:33.444
A precise definition makes later examples
easier to evaluate and revise.

19
00:01:33.554 --> 00:01:39.782
With the foundation in place, examine how
Traditional Statistical Retrieval Models

20
00:01:39.782 --> 00:01:42.613
operates in a concrete sequence.

21
00:01:42.613 --> 00:01:50.976
Topics include search interfaces, traditional
retrieval models (for example, TF-IDF, BM25),

22
00:01:50.976 --> 00:01:59.338
modern retrieval techniques (for example,
neural reranking and retrieval), search engine

23
00:01:59.338 --> 00:02:06.181
evaluation, and search applications (for
example, conversational IR, enterprise

24
00:02:06.181 --> 00:02:06.941
search).

25
00:02:06.941 --> 00:02:13.197
Consider this worked case: In a TF-IDF
calculation, the term 'machine' receives a

26
00:02:13.197 --> 00:02:20.897
high score in a short technical manual but a
low score in a lengthy general encyclopedia

27
00:02:20.897 --> 00:02:21.378
article.

28
00:02:21.378 --> 00:02:28.441
Follow every arrow in order and name the state
before and after each transition.

29
00:02:28.441 --> 00:02:34.113
The trace converts an abstract statement into
an inspectable sequence.

30
00:02:34.226 --> 00:02:42.652
Next, compare the choices involved in BM25
Ranking Formula and make the decision criteria

31
00:02:42.652 --> 00:02:43.254
explicit.

32
00:02:43.254 --> 00:02:49.629
Queries select, join, aggregate, order, and
transform data.

33
00:02:49.629 --> 00:02:57.750
Consider this worked case: BM25 assigns higher
relevance scores to documents where the query

34
00:02:57.750 --> 00:03:03.550
term appears near the beginning, reflecting
stronger user intent alignment.

35
00:03:03.551 --> 00:03:10.850
Compare alternatives against the same
constraints so the evaluation does not move

36
00:03:10.850 --> 00:03:12.066
the goalposts.

37
00:03:12.066 --> 00:03:18.769
Using shared criteria turns a preference into
a defensible technical choice.

38
00:03:18.769 --> 00:03:25.884
Name each dependency explicitly, and keep
labels and representations consistent

39
00:03:25.884 --> 00:03:28.019
throughout the analysis.

40
00:03:28.130 --> 00:03:35.917
Apply Modern Neural Retrieval Techniques to a
worked situation instead of treating it as an

41
00:03:35.917 --> 00:03:36.955
isolated definition.

42
00:03:36.955 --> 00:03:44.113
Supervised methods learn mappings from
examples paired with target labels, while

43
00:03:44.113 --> 00:03:48.017
unsupervised methods identify structure
without targets.

44
00:03:48.017 --> 00:03:55.983
Consider this worked case: A neural reranker
takes a list of initially retrieved documents

45
00:03:55.983 --> 00:04:02.810
and reorders them by predicting the
probability that each document satisfies the

46
00:04:02.810 --> 00:04:04.517
user's underlying intent.

47
00:04:04.517 --> 00:04:12.032
Work from the concrete inputs to the result,
checking units, types, or invariants at each

48
00:04:12.032 --> 00:04:12.533
step.

49
00:04:12.533 --> 00:04:17.814
The worked case shows both how to apply the
method and how to check it.

50
00:04:17.930 --> 00:04:24.072
Examine the tradeoffs in Search Engine
Evaluation Metrics, including what improves

51
00:04:24.072 --> 00:04:26.864
and what becomes more difficult.

52
00:04:26.864 --> 00:04:32.645
An introduction to the theories and techniques
used to construct search engines.

53
00:04:32.645 --> 00:04:39.466
Consider this worked case: Precision at K
measures the proportion of relevant documents

54
00:04:39.466 --> 00:04:46.286
among the top K results, providing a direct
measure of immediate query satisfaction.

55
00:04:46.286 --> 00:04:53.020
State the benefit, the cost, and the condition
under which the tradeoff is acceptable.

56
00:04:53.020 --> 00:05:00.176
Making the tradeoff visible prevents a local
improvement from hiding a larger cost.

57
00:05:00.290 --> 00:05:06.837
Stress-test the reasoning around Search
Applications and Conversational IR with an

58
00:05:06.837 --> 00:05:12.193
edge case and identify which assumption
controls the outcome.

59
00:05:12.193 --> 00:05:18.824
Students will also review current IR research,
provide written summaries of current research

60
00:05:18.824 --> 00:05:26.475
papers, and complete a group project in which
they will design and execute experiments for

61
00:05:26.475 --> 00:05:28.005
search engine components.

62
00:05:28.005 --> 00:05:36.296
Consider this worked case: A conversational IR
system maintains session state to resolve

63
00:05:36.296 --> 00:05:42.036
pronouns and follow-up questions, improving
relevance across multiple turns.

64
00:05:42.036 --> 00:05:48.614
Change one assumption at a time and observe
which part of the reasoning no longer holds.

65
00:05:48.614 --> 00:05:55.786
The edge case reveals the boundary of the
claim without discarding the useful core idea.

66
00:05:55.898 --> 00:06:03.518
Use Data Quality and Retrieval Integrity to
diagnose a failure by separating symptoms,

67
00:06:03.518 --> 00:06:05.863
causes, and corrective action.

68
00:06:05.863 --> 00:06:12.713
Data quality analysis makes missingness,
duplicates, inconsistent representation,

69
00:06:12.713 --> 00:06:18.707
integrity constraints, sampling, and update
behavior explicit.

70
00:06:18.707 --> 00:06:26.814
Consider this worked case: Before indexing, a
preprocessing step removes duplicate document

71
00:06:26.814 --> 00:06:32.894
entries and standardizes formatting to prevent
skewed ranking results.

72
00:06:32.894 --> 00:06:40.641
Locate the first point where observed behavior
diverges from expected behavior, then test the

73
00:06:40.641 --> 00:06:42.301
smallest plausible cause.

74
00:06:42.301 --> 00:06:49.582
The diagnosis is strongest when another person
can reproduce the observation and the test.

75
00:06:49.706 --> 00:06:57.334
Connect Evaluation and Error Analysis to the
earlier ideas and explain how the pieces work

76
00:06:57.334 --> 00:06:58.859
as one system.

77
00:06:58.859 --> 00:07:07.704
Evaluation must match the task and the cost of
errors, while distribution shift, missing

78
00:07:07.704 --> 00:07:13.390
data, overfitting, and unexamined sampling
choices can invalidate conclusions.

79
00:07:13.390 --> 00:07:20.941
Consider this worked case: A retrieval model
trained on technical documents may fail on

80
00:07:20.941 --> 00:07:27.413
general queries due to distribution shift,
requiring separate validation sets for each

81
00:07:27.413 --> 00:07:27.952
domain.

82
00:07:27.952 --> 00:07:37.488
Explain each connection explicitly and verify
that information retains the same meaning

83
00:07:37.488 --> 00:07:39.077
across representations.

84
00:07:39.077 --> 00:07:46.359
The synthesis matters only if every connection
remains supported by the original evidence.

85
00:07:46.466 --> 00:07:53.696
Finish with Practical Analysis Checklist as a
practical checklist that can guide a new

86
00:07:53.696 --> 00:07:54.212
analysis.

87
00:07:54.212 --> 00:08:00.978
Preprocessing must be fitted only on permitted
training data to avoid leakage.

88
00:08:00.978 --> 00:08:09.547
Consider this worked case: A group project
requires defining a baseline retrieval model,

89
00:08:09.547 --> 00:08:15.479
selecting a validation split, running
cross-validation, and documenting how

90
00:08:15.479 --> 00:08:18.775
preprocessing choices affected final rankings.

91
00:08:18.775 --> 00:08:27.008
For each checklist item, record the evidence,
the remaining uncertainty, and the next

92
00:08:27.008 --> 00:08:28.275
verification step.

93
00:08:28.275 --> 00:08:35.100
The result is a repeatable procedure, not a
conclusion that depends on memory or

94
00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:35.587
intuition.
